//! The kernel log capability.
//!
//! The kernel log is how early programs, before there are consoles and
//! log services (Phase 6), report what they do: `debug_log` writes a line
//! to it, prefixed with the thread's name.
//!
//! Programs read the log through _/devices/kmsg_ (radix-files(7)), as
//! Linux's read _/dev/kmsg_: each read of it returns one record, the
//! oldest the reader has not read, as a line of text
//! `priority,sequence,microseconds,-;text`. The priority is the record's
//! syslog(3) level (3 for errors, 4 for warnings, 6 for information, 7
//! for debugging) plus its facility times eight: the kernel's, 0, for the
//! kernel's own records, and for the lines programs wrote the facility
//! their `<N>` gave, or the user facility, 1, as Linux marks the lines
//! written to its _/dev/kmsg_; authpriv, 10, marks a security event,
//! which logd(8) keeps in the audit log if the kernel or Spark wrote it.
//! A program's line names the process that wrote it and the user that
//! process acts for after the flags, as Linux's `caller=` field does:
//! `priority,sequence,microseconds,-,process=N,user=N;text`, its text
//! starting with the writing thread's name. The kernel sets both, so
//! logd(8) can tell Spark's lines from the rest, and show each user the
//! lines of their own programs. The sequence numbers
//! every record since boot, and the microseconds are the time since boot.
//! A read waits for a record unless the file is non-blocking, and fails
//! with invalid_argument if the buffer cannot hold the line; one that
//! finds its next record gone from the kernel's ring fails with gone
//! (EPIPE) once, then goes on from the oldest still kept. Seeking to the
//! start goes back to the oldest record kept since the log was last
//! cleared, and to the end skips all of them; no other offset is taken.
//! Writing a line logs it, as `debug_log` does, at the level and facility
//! a `<N>` before it gives, or information of the user facility.
//! _/devices/kmsg_ is root's, mode 0622: root reads it, and anyone may
//! write a line to it (ADR 0252), as Linux's with `dmesg_restrict` set.

module kernel_log;

/// The most bytes of one line `debug_log` keeps.
const WRITE_MAX: u64 = 256;

/// radix-file(2)'s `control` request on _/devices/kmsg_ that clears the log
/// (as `dmesg -C` does): readers that open it or seek to its start
/// later skip the records it holds; a security event of the kernel's
/// then says which process cleared it. Needs the read and write rights,
/// so only a reader of the log, root, clears it.
const CLEAR: u32 = 0x4b4d0001;

/// The kernel log. Writing needs the write right.
interface kernel_log = 0x0009 version 1 {
    kind = kernel_log;
}
